WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:11.260 --> 00:00:15.020
&gt;&gt; Well Dawn, thanks
for joining us today.

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:16.791
It's great to have you out for

00:00:16.791 --> 00:00:21.460
your MSR AI Distinguished
Lecture which you just gave.

00:00:21.461 --> 00:00:24.230
Now in a smaller room

00:00:24.231 --> 00:00:26.360
by a fire here we
can talk a bit more

00:00:26.361 --> 00:00:31.130
personally about your career
and your current role.

00:00:31.131 --> 00:00:32.570
What's your current
position and how

00:00:32.571 --> 00:00:34.710
would you describe
what you do daily?

00:00:34.711 --> 00:00:39.080
&gt;&gt; At Uber I'm leading
data science for maps.

00:00:39.081 --> 00:00:43.640
So that's all of your experience
as a writer of the map,

00:00:43.641 --> 00:00:46.100
from the time when you open
up the app all the way to

00:00:46.101 --> 00:00:46.910
the time when you get to

00:00:46.911 --> 00:00:48.770
your destination and
get out of the car.

00:00:48.771 --> 00:00:50.810
Similarly on the driver side,

00:00:50.811 --> 00:00:53.810
turn by turn navigation
for drivers for example.

00:00:53.811 --> 00:00:56.900
So every aspect of the map
that's rider facing and that's

00:00:56.901 --> 00:00:59.810
driver facing is part
of this mapping system,

00:00:59.811 --> 00:01:02.585
but then also some things
that may not be as obvious.

00:01:02.586 --> 00:01:05.825
For example, the
mapping system predicts

00:01:05.826 --> 00:01:07.910
the travel time for drivers

00:01:07.911 --> 00:01:10.030
from point A to Point
B for any A and B,

00:01:10.031 --> 00:01:14.795
and that's used when you
request a ride as a rider

00:01:14.796 --> 00:01:17.300
we look at the different cars
that are available and see

00:01:17.301 --> 00:01:20.180
how long would it take
that driver to reach you.

00:01:20.181 --> 00:01:23.030
So predicting that travel
time in the road network is

00:01:23.031 --> 00:01:24.290
a really important output of

00:01:24.291 --> 00:01:26.530
our mapping system that
may not be as visible.

00:01:26.531 --> 00:01:29.415
&gt;&gt; It is cool. Just now during
the talk you just gave,

00:01:29.416 --> 00:01:31.970
you talked about how it's
not just machine learning,

00:01:31.971 --> 00:01:33.170
it's not just prediction.

00:01:33.171 --> 00:01:35.870
But the job you're in right now I

00:01:35.871 --> 00:01:38.720
would say at one of the most

00:01:38.721 --> 00:01:42.030
well-known and premier
companies tryiong

00:01:42.031 --> 00:01:45.440
to do its work to master
the shared economy comes at

00:01:45.441 --> 00:01:47.080
the intersection of economics and

00:01:47.081 --> 00:01:52.700
statistics and
logistics optimization.

00:01:52.701 --> 00:01:55.240
So many different fields are
coming together for you.

00:01:55.241 --> 00:01:58.550
&gt;&gt; Yeah, that's right. It's
an exciting opportunity to

00:01:58.551 --> 00:02:00.470
use my interest in

00:02:00.471 --> 00:02:03.440
statistics and machine
learning and optimization.

00:02:03.441 --> 00:02:05.810
Economics is a new field
for me in terms

00:02:05.811 --> 00:02:10.385
of economic models that
are important in pricing.

00:02:10.386 --> 00:02:12.665
So, it's been really fun
to learn from some of

00:02:12.666 --> 00:02:17.705
the top economists in the field
who have joined the team.

00:02:17.706 --> 00:02:20.285
&gt;&gt; You came to Uber

00:02:20.286 --> 00:02:24.230
after a trajectory of going

00:02:24.231 --> 00:02:28.580
from high school to Stanford
chemical engineering is it?

00:02:28.581 --> 00:02:32.195
It's a Math degree,

00:02:32.196 --> 00:02:38.580
Math and CS, and then off
to Duke to do statistics,

00:02:38.581 --> 00:02:41.560
then to Cornell as a professor,

00:02:42.800 --> 00:02:46.560
and then through
Microsoft Research visiting

00:02:46.561 --> 00:02:48.080
us and working with us
on predictive models

00:02:48.081 --> 00:02:50.640
for traffic among other things,

00:02:50.780 --> 00:02:54.140
to now in this incredible
position where you're bringing

00:02:54.141 --> 00:02:56.795
together many of the pieces
and experiences you've had.

00:02:56.796 --> 00:02:59.570
Now probably as a professor,

00:02:59.571 --> 00:03:04.000
given what you've seen going
on in your daily life,

00:03:04.001 --> 00:03:06.670
the kind of job that
you have at Uber,

00:03:06.671 --> 00:03:07.945
and the people you work with,

00:03:07.946 --> 00:03:10.865
what's the future curriculum for

00:03:10.866 --> 00:03:15.825
students to master this world?

00:03:15.826 --> 00:03:18.130
&gt;&gt; So, I absolutely think

00:03:18.131 --> 00:03:22.730
that training students
in being data

00:03:22.731 --> 00:03:26.420
driven and driving decisions

00:03:26.421 --> 00:03:30.500
through insights from data
is a critical skill set,

00:03:30.501 --> 00:03:32.615
the most critical skill set

00:03:32.616 --> 00:03:36.530
that I look for when
I'm making a hire.

00:03:36.531 --> 00:03:38.150
So in addition to

00:03:38.151 --> 00:03:40.525
being able to build a
machine learning model,

00:03:40.526 --> 00:03:43.720
this individual needs to be
able to engage with data,

00:03:43.721 --> 00:03:44.745
they need to love data,

00:03:44.746 --> 00:03:47.120
they need to when

00:03:47.121 --> 00:03:49.130
they go to make
a decision their instinct

00:03:49.131 --> 00:03:51.230
should be to look at
the data first and

00:03:51.231 --> 00:03:53.795
understand what's going well
and what's going wrong.

00:03:53.796 --> 00:03:56.510
It's a difficult skill
to teach but

00:03:56.511 --> 00:03:59.330
I think more hands on analysis

00:03:59.331 --> 00:04:02.935
which is neglected a bit in

00:04:02.936 --> 00:04:05.525
courses even these days

00:04:05.526 --> 00:04:08.465
because I think it takes more
time from the professors.

00:04:08.466 --> 00:04:11.570
So lots of hands
on data analysis.

00:04:11.571 --> 00:04:13.580
Data analysis using some of

00:04:13.581 --> 00:04:16.010
these very large scale
industrial data sets

00:04:16.011 --> 00:04:18.155
that are now available
in the public sphere,

00:04:18.156 --> 00:04:20.580
more coding for example.

00:04:20.710 --> 00:04:24.240
&gt;&gt; Per your talk you just
gave to the larger audience,

00:04:24.241 --> 00:04:26.225
you mentioned and showed

00:04:26.226 --> 00:04:28.835
these pieces coming together
in interesting novel ways.

00:04:28.836 --> 00:04:33.070
Economics, optimization,
prediction.

00:04:33.071 --> 00:04:38.030
Is there a new kind of
core coursework that's needed.

00:04:38.031 --> 00:04:39.950
It seemed like it goes
on beyond data science.

00:04:39.951 --> 00:04:41.240
It could be something

00:04:41.241 --> 00:04:43.640
about that might
resonate deeply with

00:04:43.641 --> 00:04:45.950
companies like Uber and
other companies coming to

00:04:45.951 --> 00:04:49.385
the fore that need skills,

00:04:49.386 --> 00:04:52.565
you might say, applied

00:04:52.566 --> 00:04:54.620
across disciplines jointly at

00:04:54.621 --> 00:04:56.055
the same time to solve problems.

00:04:56.056 --> 00:04:59.465
&gt;&gt; Right. Well it is important

00:04:59.466 --> 00:05:00.800
for students to come in with

00:05:00.801 --> 00:05:03.160
a basic foundation in
these different areas.

00:05:03.161 --> 00:05:04.340
My undergraduate degree was

00:05:04.341 --> 00:05:05.710
great for this because we learned

00:05:05.711 --> 00:05:07.880
some economics and
some optimization

00:05:07.881 --> 00:05:09.740
and some statistics and
some machine learning,

00:05:09.741 --> 00:05:11.330
so I would say that
that's very important,

00:05:11.331 --> 00:05:13.795
just a basic understanding
of these different areas.

00:05:13.796 --> 00:05:17.010
But we really don't expect
anybody to be coming in

00:05:17.011 --> 00:05:20.940
with being an expert in all
four or even two of those.

00:05:20.941 --> 00:05:25.020
Instead we pull together
teams who are people who have

00:05:25.021 --> 00:05:27.600
a broad set with a little bit of

00:05:27.601 --> 00:05:30.330
understanding and then
a lot of depth in one area.

00:05:30.331 --> 00:05:31.890
We create these
diverse teams with

00:05:31.891 --> 00:05:35.280
different specialties and
then we try to get them to

00:05:35.281 --> 00:05:37.950
talk to each other and
to talk a language that

00:05:37.951 --> 00:05:39.675
the other person can understand

00:05:39.676 --> 00:05:41.855
and to bounce ideas
off of each other.

00:05:41.856 --> 00:05:44.040
This is how we get

00:05:44.041 --> 00:05:46.620
the best decisions at
the end of the day.

00:05:46.621 --> 00:05:50.160
&gt;&gt; I was looking at
your previous history

00:05:50.161 --> 00:05:52.470
and we've chatted in

00:05:52.471 --> 00:05:54.255
the past about
your career and so on.

00:05:54.256 --> 00:05:56.690
I'm just curious. How
did you get involved in

00:05:56.691 --> 00:05:59.095
just doing a Math major,

00:05:59.096 --> 00:06:00.470
Math and CS at Stanford?

00:06:00.471 --> 00:06:01.740
When you were in high school did

00:06:01.741 --> 00:06:04.365
you always know you
liked math? How'd that?

00:06:04.366 --> 00:06:06.220
&gt;&gt; I always loved math.

00:06:06.221 --> 00:06:06.576
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:06:06.576 --> 00:06:12.750
&gt;&gt; Actually it was
an internship at Rockwell

00:06:12.751 --> 00:06:16.020
scientific where I was
doing machine learning

00:06:16.021 --> 00:06:19.575
research in undergraduate
that got me inspired.

00:06:19.576 --> 00:06:22.165
So actually at that
time I was a major

00:06:22.166 --> 00:06:24.810
in Chemical Engineering and after

00:06:24.811 --> 00:06:27.730
the internship I
decided to switch to

00:06:27.731 --> 00:06:31.530
Math and Comp Sci as a focus.

00:06:31.531 --> 00:06:36.600
Eventually that took me to
getting a PhD in Statistics

00:06:36.601 --> 00:06:39.570
and continuing in
the field and circling

00:06:39.571 --> 00:06:43.170
back into Tech and Data
Science these days.

00:06:43.171 --> 00:06:46.625
It all started back in
an internship with with what was

00:06:46.626 --> 00:06:51.720
a Machine Learning Lab
for defense contractors.

00:06:51.721 --> 00:06:53.095
&gt;&gt; Ruppel Science
Center in Palo Alto.

00:06:53.096 --> 00:06:54.250
&gt;&gt; That's right. That's right.

00:06:54.251 --> 00:06:55.720
&gt;&gt; I knew the place
very well doing

00:06:55.721 --> 00:06:59.290
my graduate work at Stanford.
Who'd you work with there?

00:06:59.291 --> 00:07:01.045
&gt;&gt; Mark Piot.

00:07:01.046 --> 00:07:03.090
&gt;&gt; Oh fabulous. Well
he's a mutual friend

00:07:03.091 --> 00:07:04.700
and I worked very

00:07:04.701 --> 00:07:06.570
closele with him as well
for a number of years

00:07:06.571 --> 00:07:08.910
when I was down at
Stanford Graduate School.

00:07:08.911 --> 00:07:10.590
What did you do at
Ruppel Science Center?

00:07:10.591 --> 00:07:11.595
Do you remember?

00:07:11.596 --> 00:07:16.980
&gt;&gt; I was creating
systems for training and

00:07:16.981 --> 00:07:19.470
inference in Basian networks

00:07:19.471 --> 00:07:23.490
that could be used for a variety
of different purposes.

00:07:23.491 --> 00:07:26.250
They had a lot of
different contracts

00:07:26.251 --> 00:07:28.875
at the time for different
military applications, yeah.

00:07:28.876 --> 00:07:31.110
&gt;&gt; It wasn't all military.
They had the space shuttle,

00:07:31.111 --> 00:07:32.690
they had a bunch of other
things. It was great.

00:07:32.691 --> 00:07:35.880
They set up shop right near
Stanford campus and a number

00:07:35.881 --> 00:07:37.350
of us used to stop by there and

00:07:37.351 --> 00:07:39.555
get a taste of this other world.

00:07:39.556 --> 00:07:42.310
There was a big rise of
interest in those days as I

00:07:42.311 --> 00:07:43.740
recall in decision theory and

00:07:43.741 --> 00:07:45.795
decision making and
the uncertainty.

00:07:45.796 --> 00:07:47.730
That's great. So that
moved you over to

00:07:47.731 --> 00:07:50.160
math and you probably saw
some real world aspects of it.

00:07:50.161 --> 00:07:51.210
&gt;&gt; That's right.

00:07:51.211 --> 00:07:53.730
&gt;&gt; Then when you were getting
ready for grad school,

00:07:53.731 --> 00:07:55.835
what was your decision
making like?

00:07:55.836 --> 00:07:59.850
&gt;&gt; So actually I had
a decision to make between

00:07:59.851 --> 00:08:01.495
going to graduate school in

00:08:01.496 --> 00:08:04.020
Statistics and doing it
in Computer Science.

00:08:04.021 --> 00:08:05.490
I had done a couple
of internships at

00:08:05.491 --> 00:08:07.605
that point when I was
making the decision of

00:08:07.606 --> 00:08:10.380
an internship with
Moises Goldschmidt

00:08:10.381 --> 00:08:13.290
after the internship
at Ruppel Scientific,

00:08:13.291 --> 00:08:18.300
Moises was of course a researcher
at MSR in later years.

00:08:18.301 --> 00:08:22.830
But in the end

00:08:22.831 --> 00:08:24.820
it was six in one and
half dozen the other.

00:08:24.821 --> 00:08:26.750
There was a lot of
great work going on

00:08:26.751 --> 00:08:29.400
in Artificial Intelligence
and Machine Learning

00:08:29.401 --> 00:08:30.735
at the time and

00:08:30.736 --> 00:08:34.770
also I wanted to
understand some of

00:08:34.771 --> 00:08:36.810
the foundations of statistical
learning that had been

00:08:36.811 --> 00:08:39.750
developed over
the previous 150 years.

00:08:39.751 --> 00:08:42.750
So I ultimately decided
to get a statistics PhD.

00:08:42.751 --> 00:08:44.310
&gt;&gt; It's great foundations.

00:08:44.311 --> 00:08:46.160
&gt;&gt; Yeah, absolutely. It's
important to understand.

00:08:46.161 --> 00:08:48.660
I think sometimes in
machine learning we

00:08:48.661 --> 00:08:51.570
reinvent some of the concepts
that were learned early on,

00:08:51.571 --> 00:08:52.875
but it's also true that

00:08:52.876 --> 00:08:55.080
the machine learning field
has done, I would say,

00:08:55.081 --> 00:08:56.610
a much better job of being

00:08:56.611 --> 00:08:58.690
ambitious in terms of
its problem choice.

00:08:58.691 --> 00:09:01.620
I think statistics is a little
bit more conservative as

00:09:01.621 --> 00:09:04.770
a community and wants

00:09:04.771 --> 00:09:08.040
to really ground the assumptions

00:09:08.041 --> 00:09:09.570
that statisticians are making in

00:09:09.571 --> 00:09:12.270
their models and make sure

00:09:12.271 --> 00:09:13.410
that they want to be really

00:09:13.411 --> 00:09:15.090
careful about the assumptions,

00:09:15.091 --> 00:09:16.680
whereas machine
learning researchers

00:09:16.681 --> 00:09:17.970
are often more ambitious

00:09:17.971 --> 00:09:19.410
about the difficulty and

00:09:19.411 --> 00:09:21.660
complexity of
the problems they tackle.

00:09:21.661 --> 00:09:24.820
So the statistics community

00:09:24.821 --> 00:09:26.010
and the machine learning
community really

00:09:26.011 --> 00:09:27.235
have a lot to teach each other.

00:09:27.236 --> 00:09:32.890
&gt;&gt; Well it comes down
to choice of problems,

00:09:32.891 --> 00:09:36.300
aesthetics but it really is
all statistics, as we know.

00:09:36.301 --> 00:09:38.650
&gt;&gt; I'm glad you feel that way.

00:09:38.651 --> 00:09:43.280
&gt;&gt; So this area that you are now.

00:09:43.281 --> 00:09:45.740
So I'm just going
to jump into Uber

00:09:45.741 --> 00:09:48.495
because I think it's a really
interesting topic Kerry,

00:09:48.496 --> 00:09:51.190
this whole idea of

00:09:51.191 --> 00:09:54.795
the sharing economy and

00:09:54.796 --> 00:09:58.880
scale and the ability to
collect all this data,

00:09:58.881 --> 00:10:01.310
most hopefully which is
anonymized typically.

00:10:01.311 --> 00:10:03.200
But still, the opportunity to

00:10:03.201 --> 00:10:06.175
do optimization and prediction.

00:10:06.176 --> 00:10:07.560
It's such an exciting world.

00:10:07.561 --> 00:10:09.325
I want to hear your sense
for jumping into

00:10:09.326 --> 00:10:12.380
the Uber world from
your university position.

00:10:12.381 --> 00:10:16.125
&gt;&gt; Right. Well
the most amazing thing,

00:10:16.126 --> 00:10:20.780
what is being able to
go from an idea to

00:10:20.781 --> 00:10:25.130
deployment of that idea
in a couple of months.

00:10:25.131 --> 00:10:28.250
And so getting really fast
feedback about how well

00:10:28.251 --> 00:10:31.820
an idea works in practice
at very large scale.

00:10:31.821 --> 00:10:34.540
And of course in academia,

00:10:34.541 --> 00:10:36.910
you have much more
time to develop

00:10:36.911 --> 00:10:39.995
the algorithms and to go very,

00:10:39.996 --> 00:10:42.340
very in-depth on
a particular problem space,

00:10:42.341 --> 00:10:45.560
but the timescales for
deployment and getting

00:10:45.561 --> 00:10:47.345
feedback about how
well your systems are

00:10:47.346 --> 00:10:49.170
working are much longer.

00:10:49.171 --> 00:10:51.725
So, that was very exciting
for me coming into

00:10:51.726 --> 00:10:55.030
tech and of course since
I'm leading a team,

00:10:55.031 --> 00:10:56.705
there's those individuals are

00:10:56.706 --> 00:10:58.750
working across a variety
of different spaces

00:10:58.751 --> 00:11:02.750
within maps and
market place at Uber,

00:11:02.751 --> 00:11:04.230
and each of those individuals

00:11:04.231 --> 00:11:06.610
is developing and
deploying algorithms.

00:11:06.611 --> 00:11:09.190
And so, just to see
us move as a team

00:11:09.191 --> 00:11:11.980
so quickly and to learn from

00:11:11.981 --> 00:11:14.095
that and to understand
what types of

00:11:14.096 --> 00:11:15.200
algorithms work well in

00:11:15.201 --> 00:11:17.475
particular situations
is really exciting.

00:11:17.476 --> 00:11:19.185
It's expansive too because,

00:11:19.186 --> 00:11:20.560
as I mentioned in the talk,

00:11:20.561 --> 00:11:23.640
we pull algorithms from
a variety of different areas

00:11:23.641 --> 00:11:26.715
economic models,
optimization model, statistics,

00:11:26.716 --> 00:11:29.210
and machine learning
and for example,

00:11:29.211 --> 00:11:32.070
if you look at
the the pricing space,

00:11:32.071 --> 00:11:34.995
there's literature on
pricing both coming from

00:11:34.996 --> 00:11:37.250
the optimization literature and

00:11:37.251 --> 00:11:39.420
coming from the
economic literature.

00:11:39.421 --> 00:11:41.630
And so, we hired some economists

00:11:41.631 --> 00:11:43.970
and hired some people
from operations research,

00:11:43.971 --> 00:11:45.780
and we put them on
the same team and people

00:11:45.781 --> 00:11:48.065
were just coming up with ideas

00:11:48.066 --> 00:11:52.850
and trying them and
seeing which worked best.

00:11:52.851 --> 00:11:56.300
Of course with
safeguards in place.

00:11:56.301 --> 00:11:58.160
&gt;&gt; I really think you know how

00:11:58.161 --> 00:12:00.105
lucky Uber is to have
someone like you.

00:12:00.106 --> 00:12:02.870
You know, I've looked at

00:12:02.871 --> 00:12:05.550
your work over the years
and I was like, "Wow.

00:12:05.551 --> 00:12:08.730
She's been working on
optimizing where ambulances

00:12:08.731 --> 00:12:12.020
sit in a city to
minimize their time

00:12:12.021 --> 00:12:15.295
to transport
potential accidents to

00:12:15.296 --> 00:12:17.945
the right hospitals using

00:12:17.946 --> 00:12:21.050
prediction times and
urgencies of various kinds."

00:12:21.051 --> 00:12:25.280
You know, kind of interesting
important societal project.

00:12:25.281 --> 00:12:26.450
You wouldn't think
it's like, you know,

00:12:26.451 --> 00:12:28.965
mainstream necessarily,
it's very important.

00:12:28.966 --> 00:12:30.334
And all of a sudden,

00:12:30.335 --> 00:12:31.975
you're in this new world.

00:12:31.976 --> 00:12:33.280
You know, you were
doing that work before

00:12:33.281 --> 00:12:34.620
Uber even existed, right.

00:12:34.621 --> 00:12:35.725
And here you are, someone's

00:12:35.726 --> 00:12:37.230
looking at your resume saying,

00:12:37.231 --> 00:12:39.260
"Oh my god. This is
what we need for Uber."

00:12:39.261 --> 00:12:39.855
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:12:39.856 --> 00:12:42.225
&gt;&gt; Something very related.
So, it's interesting.

00:12:42.226 --> 00:12:44.035
&gt;&gt; Is incredibly closely related.

00:12:44.036 --> 00:12:48.080
These challenges around
an ambulance context-

00:12:48.081 --> 00:12:49.205
&gt;&gt; Describe that problem
a little bit.

00:12:49.206 --> 00:12:53.900
&gt;&gt; Sure. So, one of the problems
that I worked on at

00:12:53.901 --> 00:12:56.260
Cornell was about optimization of

00:12:56.261 --> 00:12:59.650
location and relocation
for ambulance algorithms.

00:12:59.651 --> 00:13:03.865
So, if you have 10 ambulances
in a city at a given time,

00:13:03.866 --> 00:13:07.590
where should you locate them
so that they can maximize

00:13:07.591 --> 00:13:08.980
the probability of reaching

00:13:08.981 --> 00:13:11.765
a potential emergency in time.

00:13:11.766 --> 00:13:15.140
Okay? Meaning before the person

00:13:15.141 --> 00:13:17.510
has severe detrimental
health impact.

00:13:17.511 --> 00:13:21.660
So, there's some sophisticated
optimization algorithms

00:13:21.661 --> 00:13:24.590
for ambulances for
these location problems

00:13:24.591 --> 00:13:25.960
and that requires inputs,

00:13:25.961 --> 00:13:28.075
which is the prediction
of where and when

00:13:28.076 --> 00:13:30.525
these emergencies are
going to take place.

00:13:30.526 --> 00:13:32.730
And you can see
the direct parallels with

00:13:32.731 --> 00:13:35.520
the ride sharing context in

00:13:35.521 --> 00:13:41.035
which you have these drivers
and the road network,

00:13:41.036 --> 00:13:43.550
you have instances of demand
where riders want to be

00:13:43.551 --> 00:13:46.320
transported from
one location to another.

00:13:46.321 --> 00:13:48.950
The ride hailing context
is more complicated

00:13:48.951 --> 00:13:51.610
than the ambulance context not
only because of the scale,

00:13:51.611 --> 00:13:52.650
and I think at one point I

00:13:52.651 --> 00:13:54.520
calculated the
difference in scale,

00:13:54.521 --> 00:13:56.675
ten to the four factor.

00:13:56.676 --> 00:13:59.460
&gt;&gt; But that's good news
for injuries.

00:13:59.461 --> 00:14:03.780
&gt;&gt; Good news for injury.
But also the fact that

00:14:03.781 --> 00:14:05.650
drivers of course have

00:14:05.651 --> 00:14:08.180
the ability to decide where
and when they want to drive,

00:14:08.181 --> 00:14:10.240
which is different from the
ambulance context in which we

00:14:10.241 --> 00:14:15.090
can tell that to the ambulance
drivers where to locate.

00:14:15.091 --> 00:14:18.820
&gt;&gt; Interesting. If I

00:14:18.821 --> 00:14:22.730
open up my Uber app and I see
a red cross Uber emergency,

00:14:22.731 --> 00:14:25.550
I'll know where that idea
came from the future.

00:14:25.551 --> 00:14:27.800
Kidding about that. So, what was

00:14:27.801 --> 00:14:30.155
the most surprising thing

00:14:30.156 --> 00:14:33.340
that you've noted in
your your career at Uber?

00:14:33.341 --> 00:14:36.600
What's come up that just
kind of didn't expect or

00:14:36.601 --> 00:14:40.120
interesting or surprising?
About the role.

00:14:40.121 --> 00:14:41.375
&gt;&gt; The most surprising thing

00:14:41.376 --> 00:14:43.095
about the role. Yeah,
that's interesting.

00:14:43.096 --> 00:14:45.050
Well, when I came to
Uber I didn't expect

00:14:45.051 --> 00:14:47.065
to be leading
a large team actually.

00:14:47.066 --> 00:14:48.970
That was a bit of
a surprise, and-

00:14:48.971 --> 00:14:50.045
&gt;&gt; How did that happen?

00:14:50.046 --> 00:14:51.150
&gt;&gt; -definitely a change.

00:14:51.151 --> 00:14:52.055
&gt;&gt; Just showed up and they?

00:14:52.056 --> 00:14:55.150
&gt;&gt; I was planning on doing

00:14:55.151 --> 00:14:57.680
an individual contributor
role on the team

00:14:57.681 --> 00:15:00.420
and I was talking to Travis,

00:15:00.421 --> 00:15:01.660
the CEO at the time,

00:15:01.661 --> 00:15:04.985
and it turned out that
there was a need for

00:15:04.986 --> 00:15:08.550
a broader management
role and he asked

00:15:08.551 --> 00:15:12.970
me and and if there's
one principle in my career it's,

00:15:12.971 --> 00:15:14.960
if somebody asked you to

00:15:14.961 --> 00:15:16.640
do something that
you think is far

00:15:16.641 --> 00:15:21.460
beyond your capabilities,
say yes anyway.

00:15:22.220 --> 00:15:26.760
So, I just jumped in
and learned as I went.

00:15:26.761 --> 00:15:29.690
&gt;&gt; That's probably
a really good your heuristic.

00:15:29.691 --> 00:15:30.790
Except if it gets to the

00:15:30.791 --> 00:15:32.430
administrative you
want to say no quickly

00:15:32.431 --> 00:15:33.670
and figure out what

00:15:33.671 --> 00:15:36.235
level you want to be
at in an organization.

00:15:36.236 --> 00:15:39.020
It's interesting to
think about this.

00:15:39.021 --> 00:15:42.365
Do you see yourself staying in

00:15:42.366 --> 00:15:46.960
the industry for a while or
heading back to academia.

00:15:46.961 --> 00:15:50.475
How will you weigh that decision
over time do you think?

00:15:50.476 --> 00:15:51.910
I don't want an exact
answer right now,

00:15:51.911 --> 00:15:53.630
but will you just have a process?

00:15:53.631 --> 00:15:57.590
&gt;&gt; Yeah. I really love
what I'm doing right now.

00:15:57.591 --> 00:15:59.235
I love the impact.

00:15:59.236 --> 00:16:02.860
I love the immediacy
of the impact,

00:16:02.861 --> 00:16:05.285
I really enjoy the teams
that I'm working with,

00:16:05.286 --> 00:16:08.975
and the ability to hire
this incredible talent

00:16:08.976 --> 00:16:11.400
and economics and optimization

00:16:11.401 --> 00:16:13.335
and to be able to learn
from these people.

00:16:13.336 --> 00:16:15.750
So, I love what I'm doing. I
don't have any plans to go

00:16:15.751 --> 00:16:17.285
back to academia in
the near future.

00:16:17.286 --> 00:16:18.380
Maybe way down the line.

00:16:18.381 --> 00:16:21.180
&gt;&gt; Those unfortunate Cornell

00:16:21.181 --> 00:16:23.970
students who don't have
their teacher and mentor.

00:16:23.971 --> 00:16:27.105
Interesting. Do you have

00:16:27.106 --> 00:16:29.230
reflections as about your time
at Microsoft Research.

00:16:29.231 --> 00:16:33.465
You were here for a year and
a year before that as well.

00:16:33.466 --> 00:16:34.790
You worked on
some pretty wild things.

00:16:34.791 --> 00:16:37.270
you worked on
data center, forecasting,

00:16:37.271 --> 00:16:40.765
forecasting crises
in data centers.

00:16:40.766 --> 00:16:43.050
Any reflections about that work?

00:16:43.051 --> 00:16:44.420
&gt;&gt; About the data center work?

00:16:44.421 --> 00:16:45.140
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:16:45.141 --> 00:16:46.890
&gt;&gt; That's interesting, yeah.

00:16:46.891 --> 00:16:48.920
I would have loved to have seen

00:16:48.921 --> 00:16:51.470
how well it worked in practice.

00:16:51.471 --> 00:16:57.035
I think that this area of
analytics for decisions.

00:16:57.036 --> 00:16:58.620
&gt;&gt; Let's back up. So
what were you doing?

00:16:58.621 --> 00:17:02.070
&gt;&gt; Oh sure, sure. So,
the problem there was,

00:17:02.071 --> 00:17:03.735
you have a distributed large

00:17:03.736 --> 00:17:05.600
scale distributed system,
a data center,

00:17:05.601 --> 00:17:09.865
and these data centers
are instrumented.

00:17:09.866 --> 00:17:13.220
We know for each of the servers
in the data center,

00:17:13.221 --> 00:17:16.165
we have information about
a variety of metrics like

00:17:16.166 --> 00:17:21.190
CPU usage and memory
utilization and so on and so,

00:17:21.191 --> 00:17:24.300
you have a lot of data
coming out of the system and

00:17:24.301 --> 00:17:29.550
sometimes the data centers
don't hit their SLA,

00:17:29.551 --> 00:17:32.420
something goes wrong, and
you have to figure out what

00:17:32.421 --> 00:17:36.380
happened and do something
about it very, very quickly.

00:17:37.830 --> 00:17:42.240
So you have these
performance crises

00:17:42.241 --> 00:17:44.240
in the data center
and so diagnosing

00:17:44.241 --> 00:17:46.865
what went wrong is
an important challenge.

00:17:46.866 --> 00:17:51.160
We created an algorithm for
automatically detecting

00:17:51.161 --> 00:17:53.480
previous crises that looked

00:17:53.481 --> 00:17:55.430
very similar to what's
going on right now,

00:17:55.431 --> 00:17:57.590
and if you had gone back and

00:17:57.591 --> 00:17:59.170
diagnosed exactly what the cause

00:17:59.171 --> 00:18:00.365
of that previous crisis was,

00:18:00.366 --> 00:18:01.570
then you know what's
going on right

00:18:01.571 --> 00:18:03.045
now and you can do
that in a matter of

00:18:03.046 --> 00:18:04.370
minutes and then decide what

00:18:04.371 --> 00:18:05.830
the appropriate intervention is.

00:18:05.831 --> 00:18:10.070
&gt;&gt; So, something that I wrestle

00:18:10.071 --> 00:18:15.040
with all the time which
is work life balance.

00:18:15.041 --> 00:18:16.710
Given how passionate I

00:18:16.711 --> 00:18:18.525
tend to be about everything
going on and I want to get

00:18:18.526 --> 00:18:20.490
so much done and

00:18:20.491 --> 00:18:23.065
there's so many fun
and interesting projects.

00:18:23.066 --> 00:18:27.440
I can choose to go deep
on that just a few or

00:18:27.441 --> 00:18:32.210
one or pop up and do
a few things at once,

00:18:32.211 --> 00:18:37.315
but then there's family
and kids, relationships.

00:18:37.316 --> 00:18:40.180
What's your sense
for how you master

00:18:40.181 --> 00:18:42.070
work life balance and
how is it for you these

00:18:42.071 --> 00:18:44.375
days at such a fast
paced company like Uber.

00:18:44.376 --> 00:18:48.845
&gt;&gt; Right, right. I actually
find that downtime has become

00:18:48.846 --> 00:18:51.220
more important in
my current role than

00:18:51.221 --> 00:18:54.340
it was in my previous roles.

00:18:54.341 --> 00:18:57.900
So, the experience
of management is so

00:18:57.901 --> 00:18:58.985
much around being in

00:18:58.986 --> 00:19:00.110
the right headspace when you're

00:19:00.111 --> 00:19:01.370
interacting with that person.

00:19:01.371 --> 00:19:04.820
So much around inspiring people,
engaging people,

00:19:04.821 --> 00:19:11.600
helping people to thrive and
so the time that I spend

00:19:11.601 --> 00:19:14.900
with with my friends and with

00:19:14.901 --> 00:19:17.180
my family is incredibly

00:19:17.181 --> 00:19:19.050
important to being in
that right headspace.

00:19:19.051 --> 00:19:22.845
So actually, I've moved

00:19:22.846 --> 00:19:26.470
to a much more balanced approach
since coming to Uber,

00:19:26.471 --> 00:19:29.980
and I think it's really
benefited my management.

00:19:29.981 --> 00:19:32.220
&gt;&gt; It sounds like
it's very mindful

00:19:32.221 --> 00:19:35.530
as opposed to organic.

00:19:35.531 --> 00:19:38.480
You actually have a plan
to approach that.

00:19:38.481 --> 00:19:39.825
I think that's what
you're saying.

00:19:39.826 --> 00:19:41.585
It's probably something
that I need to take

00:19:41.586 --> 00:19:43.440
some advice from you
about this sometimes.

00:19:43.441 --> 00:19:45.485
&gt;&gt; Well, it's so
important to be in

00:19:45.486 --> 00:19:48.195
a positive headspace whenever

00:19:48.196 --> 00:19:52.260
we're having critical and
difficult conversations

00:19:52.261 --> 00:19:53.530
in some cases, right.

00:19:53.531 --> 00:19:55.690
And so, you have to

00:19:55.691 --> 00:19:58.495
build up this reservoir
of that positivity

00:19:58.496 --> 00:20:03.115
and it's also interesting that

00:20:03.116 --> 00:20:05.505
in other aspects
of life I think I

00:20:05.506 --> 00:20:08.065
learned lessons and bring
them back to my work.

00:20:08.066 --> 00:20:10.030
At one point I was,
I had a hobby,

00:20:10.031 --> 00:20:12.210
I was playing electric bass and I

00:20:12.211 --> 00:20:14.380
felt like some of the
things that I learned-

00:20:14.381 --> 00:20:15.115
&gt;&gt; I had no idea.

00:20:15.116 --> 00:20:16.260
&gt;&gt; -trying to. Oh,
that was a long time.

00:20:16.261 --> 00:20:18.025
&gt;&gt; Why bass?

00:20:18.026 --> 00:20:20.745
&gt;&gt; Why electric bass?
I just love it.

00:20:20.746 --> 00:20:24.810
It sounds amazing and besides
everybody needs a bassist.

00:20:24.811 --> 00:20:26.320
They don't necessarily need,

00:20:26.321 --> 00:20:27.700
everybody wants to
play the guitar,

00:20:27.701 --> 00:20:29.305
everybody wants to
play the drums.

00:20:29.306 --> 00:20:30.450
Not that many people want to

00:20:30.451 --> 00:20:32.185
play the bass.
Everybody need this.

00:20:32.186 --> 00:20:33.860
&gt;&gt; I love the bass
too and that's one of

00:20:33.861 --> 00:20:36.425
my dreams is taking off
time to learn the bass.

00:20:36.426 --> 00:20:37.165
&gt;&gt; Oh really? Incredible.

00:20:37.166 --> 00:20:38.570
&gt;&gt; That's why I asked
you that question.

00:20:38.571 --> 00:20:41.700
&gt;&gt; Yeah. But there's a kind

00:20:41.701 --> 00:20:44.060
of things that I
learned from playing

00:20:44.061 --> 00:20:46.970
the bass that I've taken
back to how I interact.

00:20:46.971 --> 00:20:48.795
&gt;&gt; Was this in a band? Were
you actually in a band?

00:20:48.796 --> 00:20:51.890
&gt;&gt; Oh my gosh, this is
a long story. I don't know.

00:20:52.210 --> 00:20:55.810
Okay. I did briefly play in

00:20:55.811 --> 00:21:00.080
a church band in
North Carolina. Yeah.

00:21:00.081 --> 00:21:05.100
&gt;&gt; Oh, my God, that sounds
like serious bass duty.

00:21:05.110 --> 00:21:08.015
&gt;&gt; It was a lot of fun.

00:21:08.016 --> 00:21:10.600
There was a lesson
there that I learned

00:21:10.601 --> 00:21:13.265
around what's called
staying in the pocket.

00:21:13.266 --> 00:21:15.200
Okay. So as a musician

00:21:15.201 --> 00:21:17.780
and playing the bass it's
really important to be

00:21:17.781 --> 00:21:20.025
consistent and to
be steady and to

00:21:20.026 --> 00:21:22.655
always be that person
that people can rely on.

00:21:22.656 --> 00:21:24.030
You don't go and play

00:21:24.031 --> 00:21:26.205
some crazy bass line in
the middle of a song.

00:21:26.206 --> 00:21:27.930
That's not your job, right.

00:21:27.931 --> 00:21:29.620
So that's called
staying in the pocket.

00:21:29.621 --> 00:21:31.790
And that lesson is so
important in life and

00:21:31.791 --> 00:21:34.010
in work I think,
staying in the pocket.

00:21:34.011 --> 00:21:38.370
So just being incredibly
mindful of what is my role in

00:21:38.371 --> 00:21:41.635
this particular interaction and

00:21:41.636 --> 00:21:44.270
not going outside of that.

00:21:44.271 --> 00:21:45.980
&gt;&gt; That's really interesting.

00:21:45.981 --> 00:21:48.405
I'm thinking about whether or not

00:21:48.406 --> 00:21:51.120
you were the kind of
person that tended to

00:21:51.121 --> 00:21:53.120
stay in the pocket
before you learned

00:21:53.121 --> 00:21:55.310
about this metaphor or it has

00:21:55.311 --> 00:21:57.170
actually helped you to deal

00:21:57.171 --> 00:21:59.450
with making sure that

00:21:59.451 --> 00:22:01.565
you were a little bit
more gentle with people.

00:22:01.566 --> 00:22:03.950
&gt;&gt; Yes. I think that
that's the case,

00:22:03.951 --> 00:22:06.180
that I'm not naturally a person

00:22:06.181 --> 00:22:11.720
who listens first and thinks
carefully about that.

00:22:11.721 --> 00:22:14.320
I tend to get excited and

00:22:14.321 --> 00:22:17.050
want to get engaged
with a response.

00:22:17.051 --> 00:22:18.230
But really stepping back and

00:22:18.231 --> 00:22:21.150
understanding what my role

00:22:21.151 --> 00:22:23.540
is in this situation
is really important.

00:22:23.541 --> 00:22:25.650
&gt;&gt; So let me ask you a quick
question given what you've

00:22:25.651 --> 00:22:27.465
seen at Uber from the inside.

00:22:27.466 --> 00:22:28.465
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:22:28.466 --> 00:22:32.050
&gt;&gt; It's the personal car
going to go away?

00:22:32.051 --> 00:22:37.740
&gt;&gt; Is the personal car
going to go away?

00:22:37.741 --> 00:22:39.670
Not in the immediate future.

00:22:39.671 --> 00:22:41.510
&gt;&gt; Let's go 15, 20 years out.

00:22:41.511 --> 00:22:43.265
&gt;&gt; Fifteen, 20 years out.

00:22:43.266 --> 00:22:46.330
I think that's
definitely the vision.

00:22:46.331 --> 00:22:49.160
I think it's, and you already

00:22:49.161 --> 00:22:52.075
start seeing this where
in the high density

00:22:52.076 --> 00:22:56.000
urban cores people
are deciding to rely

00:22:56.001 --> 00:23:00.125
heavily on a combination
of ride hailing services,

00:23:00.126 --> 00:23:03.290
the bike share services,
and public transit.

00:23:03.291 --> 00:23:05.420
Now that all these options
are available,

00:23:05.421 --> 00:23:08.435
people in urban cores at least

00:23:08.436 --> 00:23:10.040
are much more than previously

00:23:10.041 --> 00:23:12.220
deciding not to buy that car.

00:23:12.221 --> 00:23:14.930
And I think we will
see this evolution.

00:23:14.931 --> 00:23:16.940
It's a slow one because in order

00:23:16.941 --> 00:23:19.020
to move over to that world,

00:23:19.021 --> 00:23:21.040
there's all these corner cases

00:23:21.041 --> 00:23:22.050
that you have to solve for.

00:23:22.051 --> 00:23:23.580
For example you have to make sure

00:23:23.581 --> 00:23:25.985
that when you have a small child,

00:23:25.986 --> 00:23:27.630
that there is a
transportation option that

00:23:27.631 --> 00:23:29.360
involves a child seat.

00:23:29.361 --> 00:23:31.800
And the ride hailing services

00:23:31.801 --> 00:23:34.210
just haven't nailed
that particular aspect, right.

00:23:34.211 --> 00:23:36.590
So then what you
end up happening,

00:23:36.591 --> 00:23:40.140
at least what I imagine
happens, is that somebody,

00:23:40.141 --> 00:23:43.610
even if they didn't decide
to own a car before,

00:23:43.611 --> 00:23:45.490
when they have
a kid and they need

00:23:45.491 --> 00:23:47.525
that child seat then

00:23:47.526 --> 00:23:49.340
they buy that car at
that point, right.

00:23:49.341 --> 00:23:51.155
And then you have the car,
once you have the car,

00:23:51.156 --> 00:23:52.180
then you're not utilizing the

00:23:52.181 --> 00:23:53.390
ride hailing services that much.

00:23:53.391 --> 00:23:55.740
So in order to really
move into that world,

00:23:55.741 --> 00:23:57.080
we really have to provide

00:23:57.081 --> 00:23:59.960
services that fill all
of these people's needs.

00:23:59.961 --> 00:24:02.380
Needs at all stages of life.

00:24:02.381 --> 00:24:04.760
And also to really leverage

00:24:04.761 --> 00:24:07.845
these multi-modal transit,
that combination,

00:24:07.846 --> 00:24:13.875
in order to create a really
price effective solution

00:24:13.876 --> 00:24:16.410
we're looking towards
a combination not just

00:24:16.411 --> 00:24:21.150
of vehicular motor vehicle
transport but also the

00:24:21.151 --> 00:24:23.370
combination with bike
sharing and with

00:24:23.371 --> 00:24:26.625
facilitating connection to
public transit and so on.

00:24:26.626 --> 00:24:29.600
&gt;&gt; I have to say that one of

00:24:29.601 --> 00:24:31.260
my long term predictions that

00:24:31.261 --> 00:24:33.700
I've made about the future of

00:24:33.701 --> 00:24:35.940
technology is that within

00:24:35.941 --> 00:24:40.440
20 years at least one
major city will close

00:24:40.441 --> 00:24:43.045
some significant part of

00:24:43.046 --> 00:24:47.880
inner core to
human driven vehicles.

00:24:47.881 --> 00:24:50.030
And it'll just be all
public micro transit,

00:24:50.031 --> 00:24:53.890
very high speed coordinated

00:24:53.891 --> 00:24:56.830
transit that will then

00:24:56.831 --> 00:25:00.480
become a demonstration for
the rest of the world.

00:25:00.481 --> 00:25:03.715
I've just had that reflection
that I can see that coming.

00:25:03.716 --> 00:25:05.695
&gt;&gt; That's interesting.
But don't you think

00:25:05.696 --> 00:25:11.310
it will be a combination
of companies

00:25:11.311 --> 00:25:15.425
providing services and
centralized infrastructure?

00:25:15.426 --> 00:25:17.810
&gt;&gt; I'm not sure. It could be

00:25:17.811 --> 00:25:20.450
one company or a city

00:25:20.451 --> 00:25:22.370
that just invests in
public micro transit,

00:25:22.371 --> 00:25:24.410
I can use that phrase.

00:25:24.550 --> 00:25:29.670
These three door
special little pod vehicles

00:25:29.671 --> 00:25:33.330
that come and go really
quickly that are interleaving.

00:25:33.331 --> 00:25:36.315
No traffic lights because of
a coordinated inner city.

00:25:36.316 --> 00:25:38.070
And if you need to drive in

00:25:38.071 --> 00:25:40.460
as a human being you'll get
a permit and you'll get

00:25:40.461 --> 00:25:41.500
a little special beacon on

00:25:41.501 --> 00:25:43.480
your car and all
the cars will part

00:25:43.481 --> 00:25:47.145
as you come driving in with
your human driven vehicle.

00:25:47.146 --> 00:25:49.340
But we'll see how
the future goes.

00:25:49.341 --> 00:25:50.690
Do you have any other kind of

00:25:50.691 --> 00:25:52.490
predictions you
want to make about

00:25:52.491 --> 00:25:55.220
the shared economy
and optimization

00:25:55.221 --> 00:25:56.830
logistics economics coming

00:25:56.831 --> 00:25:58.950
together even beyond
transportation?

00:25:58.951 --> 00:26:00.680
&gt;&gt; Beyond transportation?
Well I thought

00:26:00.681 --> 00:26:02.480
a lot about the
transportation space.

00:26:02.481 --> 00:26:03.135
&gt;&gt; Stay there then.

00:26:03.136 --> 00:26:04.590
&gt;&gt; Yes sure. I think there's

00:26:04.591 --> 00:26:09.150
these interacting revolutions
going on in transportation.

00:26:09.151 --> 00:26:11.835
And one way of summarizing
this is the acronym ACES.

00:26:11.836 --> 00:26:16.590
So automation, like
automated vehicles,

00:26:16.660 --> 00:26:19.160
connectivity, so the vehicles

00:26:19.161 --> 00:26:20.540
talking to each other and to

00:26:20.541 --> 00:26:25.470
the network, electrification,
and sharing.

00:26:26.570 --> 00:26:29.145
If you look at the efficiency

00:26:29.146 --> 00:26:32.505
of the ownership of a private car

00:26:32.506 --> 00:26:34.430
when individuals
own a private car

00:26:34.431 --> 00:26:35.440
on average they're only

00:26:35.441 --> 00:26:38.720
using that car a couple of
percentage of the time,

00:26:38.721 --> 00:26:40.955
two, three percent of the time.

00:26:40.956 --> 00:26:44.650
And so, extremely
inefficient use of

00:26:44.651 --> 00:26:46.910
the vehicle and there's
a lot of resources that go

00:26:46.911 --> 00:26:49.375
into creating that vehicle.

00:26:49.376 --> 00:26:53.850
And if you look at utilization
of the roadways is also

00:26:53.851 --> 00:26:54.990
quite low because

00:26:54.991 --> 00:26:58.520
the traffic congestion patterns
cause inefficiencies,

00:26:58.521 --> 00:27:00.800
like the waves in
traffic cause inefficiencies.

00:27:00.801 --> 00:27:01.400
And so if you look at

00:27:01.401 --> 00:27:03.820
these four revolutions
in transportation,

00:27:03.821 --> 00:27:06.800
they really build on one
another and together

00:27:06.801 --> 00:27:10.030
could create a much
more efficient picture.

00:27:10.031 --> 00:27:12.370
So, for example, the connectivity

00:27:12.371 --> 00:27:14.890
could help with these waves
and traffic that you see.

00:27:14.891 --> 00:27:18.000
If the cars are both
automated and connected,

00:27:18.001 --> 00:27:19.780
then that means
that you could have

00:27:19.781 --> 00:27:21.730
the closer spacing
between the cars

00:27:21.731 --> 00:27:23.070
like you were talking about

00:27:23.071 --> 00:27:25.590
and you could avoid
these waves in traffic.

00:27:25.591 --> 00:27:27.930
The waves in traffic are
caused by human behavior.

00:27:27.931 --> 00:27:31.240
&gt;&gt; So you see
such an inspired person.

00:27:31.241 --> 00:27:36.510
Who are the mentors in
your life? Who's inspired you?

00:27:36.511 --> 00:27:39.025
&gt;&gt; I would call it two
particular people.

00:27:39.026 --> 00:27:42.420
One is that I mentioned before,

00:27:42.421 --> 00:27:44.540
there was Moises Goldschmidt

00:27:44.541 --> 00:27:45.890
has help me throughout my career.

00:27:45.891 --> 00:27:47.120
So as a professional mentor

00:27:47.121 --> 00:27:48.360
he's been incredibly important,

00:27:48.361 --> 00:27:50.570
and I think I counted
up the number of jobs

00:27:50.571 --> 00:27:53.390
that he's gotten me at
various times, three.

00:27:53.391 --> 00:27:54.900
I think he's gotten
three different jobs

00:27:54.901 --> 00:27:56.100
and six job offers.

00:27:56.101 --> 00:27:58.320
&gt;&gt; He's a good friend. Call
out to Moises Goldschmidt.

00:27:58.321 --> 00:27:59.595
&gt;&gt; Yeah, absolutely.

00:27:59.596 --> 00:28:01.070
But if we look on
the personal side,

00:28:01.071 --> 00:28:05.000
I would say that my mom is
an incredible inspiration.

00:28:05.001 --> 00:28:10.020
She was way ahead of her time
because she got her PhD at

00:28:10.021 --> 00:28:14.910
Stanford in demography
in the late 1960s.

00:28:14.911 --> 00:28:16.890
She was one of the few women

00:28:16.891 --> 00:28:18.480
who was doing that at the time.

00:28:18.481 --> 00:28:20.910
And the attitudes towards women

00:28:20.911 --> 00:28:24.180
in academia were totally
different at that point.

00:28:24.181 --> 00:28:28.380
So she was interested in going
into an academic career.

00:28:28.381 --> 00:28:34.090
Afterwards, she asked
the head of her department,

00:28:34.091 --> 00:28:36.450
so when she was graduating
she asked the head of

00:28:36.451 --> 00:28:39.340
her department for
a reference letter.

00:28:39.341 --> 00:28:42.320
And she was pregnant

00:28:42.321 --> 00:28:44.720
at the time and he refused
because she was pregnant.

00:28:44.721 --> 00:28:46.330
So it just goes

00:28:46.331 --> 00:28:47.990
to show that the attitudes
have changed so much.

00:28:47.991 --> 00:28:49.625
&gt;&gt; That doesn't
really compute to me.

00:28:49.626 --> 00:28:51.710
It's interesting.

00:28:51.711 --> 00:28:54.365
&gt;&gt; But she thrived nonetheless.

00:28:54.366 --> 00:28:55.475
&gt;&gt; What did she go off to do?

00:28:55.476 --> 00:28:58.170
&gt;&gt; So she did a postdoc
at the East-West Center

00:28:58.171 --> 00:29:00.850
in Hawaii, again,
studying demography.

00:29:00.851 --> 00:29:04.330
She later went on to lead
the international branch of

00:29:04.331 --> 00:29:05.875
the Census Bureau in
the U.S. government

00:29:05.876 --> 00:29:09.005
for a decade or more.

00:29:09.006 --> 00:29:10.570
She was a professor at

00:29:10.571 --> 00:29:11.630
Hong Kong University of Science

00:29:11.631 --> 00:29:13.015
and Technology for
a period of time.

00:29:13.016 --> 00:29:14.320
Done incredible things throughout

00:29:14.321 --> 00:29:16.605
her career, very groundbreaking.

00:29:16.606 --> 00:29:18.710
So she was always
a person that you

00:29:18.711 --> 00:29:21.045
couldn't tell her
what she couldn't do,

00:29:21.046 --> 00:29:23.550
she was going to go out
and prove otherwise.

00:29:23.551 --> 00:29:26.030
&gt;&gt; You've inherited
some of that phenotype

00:29:26.031 --> 00:29:27.200
and genotype probably?

00:29:27.201 --> 00:29:30.035
&gt;&gt; Yeah, well I hope that I have.

00:29:30.036 --> 00:29:31.640
&gt;&gt; Well that's great to
hear about your mom.

00:29:31.641 --> 00:29:32.910
It's fabulous that when you have

00:29:32.911 --> 00:29:34.255
a parent that's so inspired.

00:29:34.256 --> 00:29:35.285
&gt;&gt; Yeah that's incredible.

00:29:35.286 --> 00:29:37.820
&gt;&gt; It's great. Well thanks
so much for coming in.

00:29:37.821 --> 00:29:39.140
It's great seeing you again.

00:29:39.141 --> 00:29:41.250
&gt;&gt; It's great to see you again.

